/usr/bin/overstrike from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for overstrike, so this is the disassembly, and overstrike.c
is that disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f overstrike.ctl overstrike.dist

overstrike.dist is the binary off inode 29 of disks/dist/1013-8_dist_3.IMD - the
third distribution disk - 3233 bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It has no
header - see ../newuser/README - and uses Whitesmith's runtime, c.ent at 0ce9
and c.ents at 0b9c.  It is the program of that name in Kernighan and Plauger's
Software Tools.

Its own code is main at 010e and nothing else.  It keeps two words in the data:
the column the output has reached (0d1b, which starts at 1) and the column the
input has (0d1d, set from the first at the top of each pass).  A pass is: read
characters with getchar for as long as they are backspaces, each taking one
off the second column, to 1 at the lowest; then, if the input is behind the
output, write a carriage return and set the output to column 1 and write blanks
until it reaches the input; then, unless it is the end of the file, write the
character and move the output on by one, or back to 1 for a newline.

So "ab", two backspaces and "__" comes out as "ab", a return and "__": the
underline is written on a second pass over the line.  And because the catching
up is done before the end of the file is looked at, a line that is backed up
when the file ends has the return and the blanks written after it.

The exit status is the -1 that the last getchar left in BC at the end of the
file, which is 255, or, when the file ends on a backspace, the byte that was
last in BC - the original's status here is whatever the last character was.
overstrike.c exits 0.

Checked in usersim against the original, byte for byte, on five thousand random
characters made of letters, blanks, backspaces, tabs, newlines, carriage
returns and bytes over 0177; on short cases of backspaces at the start, in
the middle and at the end of the file; on an empty file and on text.
